1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,531
Last year, American Airlines flew about 224 million passengers.

2
00:00:05,811 --> 00:00:08,878
It brought in a record $54.6 billion.

3
00:00:09,158 --> 00:00:12,771
And when the year was over, its net profit

4
00:00:12,771 --> 00:00:16,384
was $111 million. That's about 50 cents per passenger.

5
00:00:16,664 --> 00:00:20,449
But in that same year, credit card companies and

6
00:00:20,449 --> 00:00:22,972
other partners paid American $6.2 billion.

7
00:00:23,252 --> 00:00:25,992
That's more than 50 times its entire profit.

8
00:00:26,272 --> 00:00:28,455
So what is an airline actually selling?

9
00:00:28,455 --> 00:00:29,703
Let's follow the money.

10
00:00:30,453 --> 00:00:32,287
Quick note before we start.

11
00:00:32,287 --> 00:00:35,588
I'm an AI narrator, and the numbers in this

12
00:00:35,588 --> 00:00:38,890
video come from the airlines' and American Express's own

13
00:00:38,890 --> 00:00:41,824
annual reports and their filings with the U.S.

14
00:00:41,824 --> 00:00:43,292
Securities and Exchange Commission.

15
00:00:43,292 --> 00:00:45,492
They're all linked in the description.

16
00:00:45,772 --> 00:00:48,746
This is a video about how the business works.

17
00:00:48,746 --> 00:00:51,719
It's not financial advice, and it's not a credit

18
00:00:51,719 --> 00:00:52,380
card recommendation.

19
00:00:53,130 --> 00:00:54,432
Let's start with Delta.

20
00:00:54,712 --> 00:01:00,160
In 2025, Delta brought in $63.4 billion, and made

21
00:01:00,160 --> 00:01:03,187
$5.8 billion in operating profit.

22
00:01:03,467 --> 00:01:06,804
Operating profit is what's left from running the airline,

23
00:01:06,804 --> 00:01:08,287
before interest and taxes.

24
00:01:08,567 --> 00:01:12,401
In the same year, American Express paid Delta $8.2

25
00:01:12,401 --> 00:01:15,809
billion. That's up 11% from the year before.

26
00:01:16,089 --> 00:01:20,126
So the credit card deal brought in more than

27
00:01:20,126 --> 00:01:21,920
Delta's entire operating profit.

28
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:23,714
About $2.4 billion more.

29
00:01:23,994 --> 00:01:27,239
In its annual report, Delta calls it its most

30
00:01:27,239 --> 00:01:29,763
significant and valuable contract to sell miles.

31
00:01:30,043 --> 00:01:32,653
And it expects that number to grow to $10

32
00:01:32,653 --> 00:01:34,393
billion over the next few years.

33
00:01:34,673 --> 00:01:37,556
So why would a credit card company hand an

34
00:01:37,556 --> 00:01:39,157
airline $8 billion a year?

35
00:01:39,907 --> 00:01:41,009
Here's how it works.

36
00:01:41,289 --> 00:01:44,487
When you use a Delta credit card from American

37
00:01:44,487 --> 00:01:46,618
Express, every purchase earns you SkyMiles.

38
00:01:46,898 --> 00:01:49,508
But those miles don't come out of nowhere.

39
00:01:49,508 --> 00:01:52,443
American Express buys them from Delta, at a price

40
00:01:52,443 --> 00:01:53,748
set in their contract.

41
00:01:54,028 --> 00:01:55,608
And it's not just miles.

42
00:01:55,608 --> 00:01:58,452
The deal also pays for the perks that come

43
00:01:58,452 --> 00:02:01,296
with the card: free checked bags, lounge access, priority

44
00:02:01,296 --> 00:02:04,140
boarding, and the right to put Delta's name on

45
00:02:04,140 --> 00:02:04,456
it.

46
00:02:04,736 --> 00:02:07,701
The price of a single mile isn't public.

47
00:02:07,701 --> 00:02:09,184
But the total is:

48
00:02:09,184 --> 00:02:10,666
$8.2 billion last year.

49
00:02:10,946 --> 00:02:12,741
And it keeps climbing.

50
00:02:12,741 --> 00:02:16,779
Delta's cash sales of miles to its partners went

51
00:02:16,779 --> 00:02:20,818
from $6.9 billion in 2023, to $7.4 billion, to

52
00:02:20,818 --> 00:02:22,612
$8 billion in 2025.

53
00:02:22,892 --> 00:02:25,194
For Delta, this is close to perfect money.

54
00:02:25,194 --> 00:02:27,783
It gets paid in cash today, for a seat

55
00:02:27,783 --> 00:02:30,372
it might give you months, or even years, from

56
00:02:30,372 --> 00:02:30,660
now.

57
00:02:30,940 --> 00:02:33,747
And the airline decides which seats it releases for

58
00:02:33,747 --> 00:02:36,242
miles, and how many miles each one costs.

59
00:02:36,522 --> 00:02:40,659
In 2025, SkyMiles members cashed in miles for about

60
00:02:40,659 --> 00:02:42,498
35 million award tickets.

61
00:02:42,778 --> 00:02:45,897
Award trips made up about 12% of all the

62
00:02:45,897 --> 00:02:47,284
passenger miles Delta flew.

63
00:02:47,564 --> 00:02:50,382
And all the miles that have been sold but

64
00:02:50,382 --> 00:02:53,201
not used yet? Delta records them as a liability.

65
00:02:53,201 --> 00:02:55,080
Flights it still owes its members.

66
00:02:55,360 --> 00:02:59,547
At the end of 2025, that balance was $9.3

67
00:02:59,547 --> 00:03:00,012
billion.

68
00:03:00,292 --> 00:03:02,719
Think of it like a bank account, where you

69
00:03:02,719 --> 00:03:04,876
can only withdraw your money as plane seats.

70
00:03:04,876 --> 00:03:06,494
And the airline is the bank.

71
00:03:07,244 --> 00:03:10,325
So American Express pays Delta $8 billion a year.

72
00:03:10,325 --> 00:03:12,380
Where does that money come from?

73
00:03:12,660 --> 00:03:15,061
Start with the store where you use the card.

74
00:03:15,061 --> 00:03:17,463
Every time you pay with a credit card, the

75
00:03:17,463 --> 00:03:18,531
store pays a fee.

76
00:03:18,811 --> 00:03:23,126
Across all American Express cards, Amex kept about 2.24%

77
00:03:23,126 --> 00:03:26,963
of everything spent, according to its annual report.

78
00:03:27,243 --> 00:03:30,046
And spending on Delta cards is enormous.

79
00:03:30,046 --> 00:03:33,650
Amex says the Delta portfolio is about 13% of

80
00:03:33,650 --> 00:03:36,052
everything spent on its cards worldwide.

81
00:03:36,332 --> 00:03:41,147
Amex card members spent $1.67 trillion in 2025.

82
00:03:41,147 --> 00:03:46,563
13% of that is roughly $217 billion, on Delta

83
00:03:46,563 --> 00:03:47,767
cards alone.

84
00:03:48,047 --> 00:03:51,740
If those purchases paid the average fee, stores would

85
00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:54,202
have paid Amex about $4.9 billion.

86
00:03:54,482 --> 00:03:57,505
That's not enough to cover the $8.2 billion Amex

87
00:03:57,505 --> 00:03:58,176
paid Delta.

88
00:03:58,456 --> 00:04:01,662
The other big pieces come from the cardholders themselves:

89
00:04:01,662 --> 00:04:04,867
annual fees, and interest on the balances they don't

90
00:04:04,867 --> 00:04:05,579
pay off.

91
00:04:05,859 --> 00:04:07,740
And here's a telling number.

92
00:04:07,740 --> 00:04:11,126
Delta cards are 13% of the spending, but 21%

93
00:04:11,126 --> 00:04:14,136
of all the money card members owe Amex.

94
00:04:14,416 --> 00:04:17,991
So relative to what they spend, Delta cardholders carry

95
00:04:17,991 --> 00:04:20,771
bigger balances than the average Amex customer.

96
00:04:21,051 --> 00:04:22,334
Then there's everyone else.

97
00:04:22,334 --> 00:04:25,223
Stores usually charge the same price whether you pay

98
00:04:25,223 --> 00:04:27,148
with a card or with cash.

99
00:04:27,428 --> 00:04:31,418
A 2010 study by economists at the Federal Reserve

100
00:04:31,418 --> 00:04:35,408
Bank of Boston estimated that each household paying with

101
00:04:35,408 --> 00:04:39,397
cash was effectively sending about $149 a year to

102
00:04:39,397 --> 00:04:41,170
households paying with cards.

103
00:04:41,450 --> 00:04:44,282
The card industry disagrees with that framing.

104
00:04:44,282 --> 00:04:47,922
It argues that cards bring stores more customers and

105
00:04:47,922 --> 00:04:48,731
bigger purchases.

106
00:04:49,011 --> 00:04:51,384
Either way, the miles in your account were paid

107
00:04:51,384 --> 00:04:52,966
for by a lot of people.

108
00:04:52,966 --> 00:04:53,758
Not just you.

109
00:04:54,508 --> 00:04:57,607
The clearest proof of how valuable this business is

110
00:04:57,607 --> 00:04:58,640
came in 2020.

111
00:04:58,920 --> 00:05:01,999
When the pandemic hit, almost nobody was flying.

112
00:05:01,999 --> 00:05:05,463
In the first half of the year, Delta's passenger

113
00:05:05,463 --> 00:05:06,618
revenue fell 60%.

114
00:05:06,898 --> 00:05:09,886
But Delta told investors that its sales to American

115
00:05:09,886 --> 00:05:12,874
Express had not fallen at the same rate as

116
00:05:12,874 --> 00:05:13,538
the airline.

117
00:05:13,818 --> 00:05:16,197
Its members redeemed 78% fewer miles.

118
00:05:16,197 --> 00:05:19,368
But they kept earning them, on the ground.

119
00:05:19,648 --> 00:05:21,587
Airlines needed cash, and fast.

120
00:05:21,587 --> 00:05:25,075
And instead of only borrowing against their planes, they

121
00:05:25,075 --> 00:05:27,014
borrowed against their loyalty programs.

122
00:05:27,294 --> 00:05:31,718
United went first. It valued its MileagePlus program at

123
00:05:31,718 --> 00:05:36,142
about $21.9 billion. Roughly 12 times what the program

124
00:05:36,142 --> 00:05:37,617
earned in 2019.

125
00:05:37,897 --> 00:05:40,830
In that year, the loyalty program alone had produced

126
00:05:40,830 --> 00:05:43,110
about a quarter of United's core earnings.

127
00:05:43,390 --> 00:05:46,504
United borrowed $6.8 billion against it.

128
00:05:46,504 --> 00:05:49,617
Delta borrowed $9 billion against SkyMiles.

129
00:05:49,617 --> 00:05:53,249
And American borrowed $10 billion against AAdvantage.

130
00:05:53,529 --> 00:05:55,748
Together, that's $25.8 billion.

131
00:05:55,748 --> 00:05:59,633
Lent not against airplanes, but against points.

132
00:05:59,913 --> 00:06:02,766
The lenders were betting that even if people stopped

133
00:06:02,766 --> 00:06:04,352
flying, they wouldn't stop swiping.

134
00:06:04,632 --> 00:06:08,003
And by 2025, United had paid back every dollar

135
00:06:08,003 --> 00:06:09,876
of its loyalty program debt.

136
00:06:10,626 --> 00:06:11,752
And this isn't new.

137
00:06:11,752 --> 00:06:14,284
The card company has been backing the airline for

138
00:06:14,284 --> 00:06:15,128
a long time.

139
00:06:15,408 --> 00:06:19,258
In 2008, during the financial crisis, American Express paid

140
00:06:19,258 --> 00:06:23,108
Delta $1 billion in advance, for miles it would

141
00:06:23,108 --> 00:06:24,391
hand out later.

142
00:06:24,671 --> 00:06:29,234
It did it again from 2011 to 2014.

143
00:06:29,234 --> 00:06:31,515
$675 million, every December.

144
00:06:31,795 --> 00:06:35,546
By 2019, Delta said American Express was contributing more

145
00:06:35,546 --> 00:06:37,629
than $4 billion a year.

146
00:06:37,629 --> 00:06:40,547
Six years later, it was $8.2 billion.

147
00:06:40,547 --> 00:06:41,380
Roughly double.

148
00:06:41,660 --> 00:06:44,231
In practice, that works a lot like a loan.

149
00:06:44,231 --> 00:06:46,802
Except it gets paid back in seats instead of

150
00:06:46,802 --> 00:06:47,088
cash.

151
00:06:47,368 --> 00:06:51,263
Today, Delta's contract with American Express runs through the

152
00:06:51,263 --> 00:06:52,562
end of 2029.

153
00:06:52,842 --> 00:06:55,082
And for Amex, it's huge too.

154
00:06:55,082 --> 00:06:58,442
13% of all its card spending comes from one

155
00:06:58,442 --> 00:06:59,189
airline's card.

156
00:06:59,939 --> 00:07:02,463
Now every big airline wants more of this money.

157
00:07:02,743 --> 00:07:06,287
American moved its card deal to Citi, which became

158
00:07:06,287 --> 00:07:09,830
its only U.S. card issuer at the start of

159
00:07:09,830 --> 00:07:10,224
2026.

160
00:07:10,504 --> 00:07:13,814
American expects payments from its card and other partners

161
00:07:13,814 --> 00:07:17,123
to grow about 10% a year, until they approach

162
00:07:17,123 --> 00:07:18,594
$10 billion a year.

163
00:07:18,874 --> 00:07:22,559
It says that should add about $1.5 billion a

164
00:07:22,559 --> 00:07:25,834
year to its pre-tax profit, compared to 2024.

165
00:07:26,114 --> 00:07:29,521
That expected boost alone is more than 10 times

166
00:07:29,521 --> 00:07:31,792
American's entire net profit last year.

167
00:07:32,072 --> 00:07:34,480
Delta is aiming for $10 billion too.

168
00:07:34,760 --> 00:07:37,576
That's why the flight attendant pitches a card over

169
00:07:37,576 --> 00:07:40,392
the speaker. That's why free bags, early boarding and

170
00:07:40,392 --> 00:07:42,269
lounge access come with a card.

171
00:07:42,549 --> 00:07:44,456
The plane is the product you see.

172
00:07:44,456 --> 00:07:46,362
The card is the product that pays.

173
00:07:47,112 --> 00:07:49,774
So, are airlines really credit card companies?

174
00:07:50,054 --> 00:07:52,607
Not exactly. They still have to fly you somewhere,

175
00:07:52,607 --> 00:07:55,160
safely and on time, or none of this works.

176
00:07:55,160 --> 00:07:57,713
A mile is only worth something if there's a

177
00:07:57,713 --> 00:07:59,415
seat at the end of it.

178
00:07:59,695 --> 00:08:02,596
But some of the steadiest money in the business

179
00:08:02,596 --> 00:08:04,208
doesn't come from the flight.

180
00:08:04,208 --> 00:08:07,110
It comes from what people buy on the ground.

181
00:08:07,110 --> 00:08:09,044
Groceries, gas, dinner. All earning miles.

182
00:08:09,324 --> 00:08:11,690
Every swipe sends a few cents to an airline

183
00:08:11,690 --> 00:08:13,267
you might not fly for months.

184
00:08:13,547 --> 00:08:15,397
Do you have an airline credit card?

185
00:08:15,397 --> 00:08:17,247
And do you actually use the miles?

